//Rule of 0

The Rule of 0 says:

If your class does not manually manage resources, you should generally not write custom destructors, copy constructors, copy assignment, move constructors, or move assignment operators.

Instead, use C++'s resource-managing classes such as:

class Student {
public:
    string name;
    vector<int> marks;
};

Here, string and vector manage their own memory.

So you don't need to manually write:

Destructor
Copy Constructor
Copy Assignment
Move Constructor
Move Assignment

The compiler-generated versions handle these members correctly.

Simple comparison
Rule of 0 → Let C++ manage resources
Rule of 3 → Custom destructor + copy operations
Rule of 5 → Rule of 3 + move operations

Simple definition:

Rule of 0 = prefer using resource-managing types so your class doesn't need custom special member functions.